(i) Loss of biodiversity in an area may lead to (any two):
- a decline in plant production;
- lowered resistance to environmental perturbations such as drought;
- increased variability in certain ecosystem processes such as plant productivity, water use, and pest and disease cycles.

(ii) Any two major causes of loss of biodiversity (the "Evil Quartet"):
- habitat loss and fragmentation;
- over-exploitation;
- alien species invasions;
- co-extinctions.

The megasporangium is the **ovule**. A typical anatropous ovule is drawn in longitudinal section as an inverted oval body attached to the placenta by a stalk, with these labels (any six):
- **funicle**, the stalk attaching the ovule to the placenta;
- **hilum**, the junction between the body of the ovule and the funicle;
- **outer integument** and **inner integument**, the protective envelopes around the ovule;
- **micropyle**, the small opening at the tip left by the integuments, at the **micropylar pole**;
- **chalazal pole**, the basal part opposite the micropylar end;
- **nucellus**, the mass of cells enclosed within the integuments;
- **embryo sac** (female gametophyte), located in the nucellus.
